// @check
// @error operator '+' is not defined for i64 | boolean and i32
// CONTRACT (must-error): AN `is T` GUARD DOES NOT NARROW A LOOP BODY, while a `!= null`
// guard does (`while-body-narrowing.vl`). The asymmetry is not an oversight — it is the
// price of the write contract a narrowing carries, and it lands on the two guards
// differently.
//
// A narrowed binding's type is also what it ACCEPTS inside the scope. For a NULL STRIP the
// single assignment that becomes illegal is the one that would falsify the fact (`e =
// null`), so the restriction is exactly the soundness the guard is for. For an `is T` pin
// the assignment that becomes illegal is the one that FLIPS the variant — and a flip is
// how a loop over a union makes progress: `unions/paren-is-narrow.vl`'s `whileParen` is
// `while (x is i64) { … x = true }`, which narrowing this body turns into `cannot assign
// boolean to i64`. The cost would land on working code instead of on unsound code.
//
// The `if` ARM DOES REJECT THAT FLIP today (`if x is i64 { x = true }` is the same
// diagnostic), so the `if` spelling is not the oracle for this axis — the corpus case is,
// and it says a loop must keep accepting it. Hoist the read into an `if` arm to get the
// narrowing, exactly as before.
function f(x: i64 | boolean) {
  let r = 0
  while x is i64 {
    r = 1
    return x + 1
  }
  return r
}
print(toString(f(true)))
